 of ukraine have been learning  how to  how to transmit these weapons to the  front and it's not a secret where the  russians are at the moment and where the  russians are going to strike it's very  very clear so you know i think that the  methods are there  and the destination is really not  ambiguous so i think it will come very  quickly to ukraine  and speaking about the ukrainian army  what chance do they have to fend off  this offensive  it's truly anybody's guess in terms of  the day-to-day military uh outcomes  they've so far done very very well in  the north but the terrain is going to be  more difficult for them in the south  it's more wide open  and the russians are changing tactics  and will likely be i think more  successful than they were in the north  but whether they can really overcome  ukraine in the south and the east  i think it's unlikely  and what's your assessment sir in terms  of this support that the u.s is  providing ukraine is it  sustainable  i think it's certainly sustainable you  see enormous will in congress that's  really where the money is coming from  it's one of the few issues on which  republicans and democrats really agree  uh in washington and i think that the  white house is very happy to have this  congressional support so i think that  there's no way that this military aid is  going to stop or slow down  in the short to medium term  a very striking comment that the  president made as well is regarding the  u.s banning russian-affiliated ships  from u.s ports how significant is that  well i don't know if in and of itself  it's hugely significant i don't think  there's a big trading relationship  between russia and the united states in  either direction but it's the slow  tightening of the noose around the  russian economy  and the gradual abrupt and gradual  isolation  of the russian economy and so as part of  this larger pattern  russia just sees markets and options  closing off  and that will over quite a long period  of time that will have a dramatic effect  on on the russian economy  michael kimmich historian and expert on  russia and ukraine sir thank you for  taking our questions greatly appreciate  it  thank you for having me  u.s president joe biden also said there  is quote no evidence that mariupol has  fallen to russia it comes after moscow  tried to claim victory there saying  russian forces have successfully  liberated the port city in southeastern  ukraine the azov style steel plant is  the last place in mariupol still in  ukrainian hands it's believed some 2 000  soldiers and around a thousand civilians  are sheltering there kiev is demanding  moscow open a humanitarian corridor to  let civilians leave  president putin called off plans to  storm the complex instead in a televised  meeting he told his defense minister to  completely seal off the area our report  now on the steel plant that has become a  symbol of ukrainian resistance  [Applause]  the vast azov style steel plant  seen here in footage from russian state  television is the last part of mariopol  and ukrainian control after nearly two  months of heavy fighting  with much of the strategic port in ruins  president putin's claimed victory in the  city  and told his defence minister to seal  off its defenders in their last  stronghold  [Applause]  i consider the proposed storming of the  industrial area pointless  i order to abort it  there is no need to climb into these  catacombs and crawl underground through  these industrial facilities  block off this industrial area so that  not even a fly can get through  ukrainian authorities have urged moscow  to open humanitarian corridors to allow  those sheltering in the heavily  bombarded azostal plant to flee  these pictures provided by the plant's  defenders show some of the hundreds of  civilians said to be sheltering in  bunkers below the steelworks  but previous efforts to organize  evacuations from mario paul have had  little success on wednesday only four  buses carrying evacuees managed to leave  the city through a humanitarian corridor  some one hundred thousand residents are  thought to still be living among the  ruins  those now blockaded in the azov style  steel plant may well face the choice  between starvation or surrender  dr aglaya snetkov is an expert on  russia's foreign and security policy and  i asked her what she thinks is behind  president putin's decision not to storm  the steel plant in mariupol  and i think effectively it's a  recognition that to storm it will be  very difficult for the russians and that  they would sustain heavy losses and that  it will take a long time so i would go  along with sort of putting um  his own assessment that essentially it's  not effective for the russian troops to  storm it lose a lot of manpower and  actually it is more effective from the  point of view of the russian troops to  block continue the blockade because  there are the ukrainians are unable to  resupply um their troops in  the um still um  exhaustion plant and therefore you know  it will be a matter of time till the  blockade effectively  um creates a situation where the  fighters will have to come out and the  russians would like to preserve their  troops they would like to move them to  dambass to link them up with their  troops in luhansk and daniels and  therefore effectively they're not  prepared to lose them  over the  the current blockade of azostal so what  happens next now what does victory look  like for the russians  i think that's sort of the you know  million dollar question we don't know  um i think commentators and i would  agree with the assessments to say that  in some ways britain can turn anything  into victory the russian public although  on the whole seems to be supporting the  war they don't seem to have a good idea  as to what a victory looks like and in  that respect i think it is down to putin  and what he wants to see as a victory  and i think that's why the situation is  so dangerous because we simply don't  know  what is enough for putin you know is a  victory over mariopol enough  is the taking over of all of the luhansk  daniels oblast  a victory is it pushing further into the  parts of eastern ukraine is it getting a  negotiation  between himself and zelensky we don't  know at this stage what is sufficient  and i think that is why  it is very dangerous  as the situation develops  dr aglaya snetkoff expert on russia's  foreign and security policy thank you  very much for taking our questions thank  you
 

